/*
 * The consumer fixture, type-checked the way the consumers actually check.
 *
 * `tsconfig.json` extends the repository base, which is `moduleResolution:
 * Bundler` — the same setting Vite, Vitest, and Storybook run under. Every repo
 * this fixture is a reference implementation *for* is on `module` /
 * `moduleResolution: Node16` or `NodeNext`, and those two resolvers disagree
 * about published declarations: Bundler accepts an extensionless relative
 * specifier inside a `"type": "module"` package, Node16 rejects it (TS2834).
 *
 * Checking only under Bundler is how `@jflamb/keycaps-react` shipped 0.1.2 with
 * sixteen unresolvable specifiers in its declaration output. Consumers all set
 * `skipLibCheck: true`, which turned those errors into silence and every root
 * export into `any` — a migration that builds, deploys, renders correctly, and
 * carries no type safety on any component prop.
 *
 * So this second pass exists to fail where the first one cannot:
 *
 * - `module` / `moduleResolution: Node16` resolves `@jflamb/keycaps-react` the
 *   way `mcp-dnsimple` and `mcp-unifi` resolve it, through the `exports` map and
 *   with ESM extension rules in force.
 * - `skipLibCheck: false` is the point. With it on, the declaration errors are
 *   suppressed and the damage is invisible. Turning it off is what makes the
 *   published `.d.ts` a checked artifact rather than an assumed one.
 *
 * `src/types.ts` carries the other half of the guard: assertions that fail if
 * the exports resolve but degrade to `any`. Those run under both configs,
 * because `any` is the failure mode `skipLibCheck: true` leaves behind.
 */
{
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "module": "Node16",
    "moduleResolution": "Node16",
    "skipLibCheck": false
  },
  "include": ["src"]
}
